# Every note a document owes on its own face. One row per (filing, note) that must be visible, not
# merely present.
#
# Owed where the default reading is wrong
#   That is the rule that decides membership: a note is owed wherever the notation's own default
#   reading is wrong. Not everything true about a filing belongs on its face. These five do,
#   because without each one a reader reaches a specific false conclusion from a correct drawing.
#   Four come from a glyph that means something else in BPMN; the fifth comes from the absence of
#   one, which is why no care in the emitter can prevent it:
#     scope       a pool with three lanes reads as *this is the system*. It is a projection, and
#                 nearly every filing here declines to claim otherwise.
#     search      an absent dependence line reads as independence. Most filings state no coupling,
#                 and the four typed answers are the only thing separating *nobody looked* from
#                 *somebody looked and found none*.
#     dependence  a dotted arrow between two lanes reads as flow. A coupling is a dependence and
#                 is never evidence for a fusion, which the schema says in capitals.
#     cover       a dashed box around nodes reads as a container. A group is a cover: an
#                 operation may be in several and is in exactly one lane.
#     lane        a lane drawn empty reads as an idle part of the system. Most lanes here are
#                 empty and correct: `rank/incidence_reach.sqlc` measures the incidence sparse
#                 and the magnitude total, so the figure is filed and BPMN has no glyph for it.
#
# `documentation` puts them on no page
#   It puts all of them in the artifact and on no page. It is an annotation admitted on any base
#   element and invisible in every rendering. `textAnnotation` is the only element in BPMN that
#   puts words on the canvas, and withholding it on the reason *documentation is spent instead* is
#   true, and about the wrong property. Two readers need two carriers: a tool reads the
#   documentation where it is attached, a person reads the note where it is drawn.
#
# Floating, with no `association`
#   An artifact may stand alone, and attaching these would spend `association` a second way, which
#   is exactly the collision that ruled it out for `F`: the two consolidation filings already carry
#   coupling lines and would carry note lines beside them, and a reader could not tell which
#   dotted line was which.
#
# Conditional on pages three, four and five
#   A document with no coupling owes no note about arrows, one with no cover owes none about dashed
#   boxes, and one whose every lane holds a node owes none about empty ones. A legend for a glyph
#   that is not there is noise, and noise is how a legend stops being read. The fifth is
#   conditional on an absence rather than a presence, which is the one arm whose population grows
#   when a filing says less.
#
# Each note names the relation it states
#   For the reason the `--` line exists. These are the only sentences in this pipeline a person
#   reads off a picture, so they are the ones furthest from the model and the ones with no other
#   route back to it. `diagrams/annotated.sqlc` carries the same column for the sentences a tool
#   reads.
#
# The fifth arm cites a different relation
#   Its population and its citation are two different relations, and the emitter's attribution
#   law is why. `diagrams/lane_occupancy.sqlc` decides who owes the note;
#   `diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc` is what the note says, and it is the one the emitter queries. A
#   `states` the emitter never read would be decorative, which that law refuses by scraping the
#   `query_file!` literals out of its own source rather than trusting this column.
#
# Defined, not read
#   A program reads these columns, so this statement defines its vocabulary in its own
#   WITH clause: a view keeps the rows and loses the column's origin, which is where
#   NOT NULL lives. assets/sqlc/views.sqlc says what a definition is.
#
-- the notes a correct reading requires; BPMN 2.0 tTextAnnotation, drawn and unattached.
WITH
notations AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/notations.sqlc)
),
part_references AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/part_references.sqlc)
),
parts AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(composition/parts.sqlc)
),
calls AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(diagrams/calls.sqlc)
),
inductions AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/inductions.sqlc)
),
categories AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(diagrams/categories.sqlc)
),
couplings AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/couplings.sqlc)
),
dependences AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(diagrams/dependences.sqlc)
),
foreign_calls AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(diagrams/foreign_calls.sqlc)
),
draws AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(entries/draws.sqlc)
),
lane_membership AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(diagrams/lane_membership.sqlc)
),
epistemics_scopes AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(epistemics/scopes.sqlc)
),
diagrams_scopes AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(diagrams/scopes.sqlc)
),
coupling_searches AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(epistemics/coupling_searches.sqlc)
),
searches AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(diagrams/searches.sqlc)
),
every_layer AS NOT MATERIALIZED (
    :define(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
)
SELECT s.filing, 'scope' AS note, 'diagrams/scopes.sqlc' AS states
FROM ( :compose(diagrams/scopes.sqlc) ) s
UNION ALL
SELECT s.filing, 'search', 'diagrams/searches.sqlc'
FROM ( :compose(diagrams/searches.sqlc) ) s
UNION ALL
SELECT DISTINCT d.filing, 'dependence', 'diagrams/dependences.sqlc'
FROM ( :compose(diagrams/dependences.sqlc) ) d
UNION ALL
SELECT DISTINCT c.filing, 'cover', 'diagrams/categories.sqlc'
FROM ( :compose(diagrams/categories.sqlc) ) c
UNION ALL
SELECT DISTINCT e.filing, 'lane', 'diagrams/lane_grain.sqlc'
FROM ( :compose(diagrams/lane_occupancy.sqlc) ) e
WHERE NOT e.occupied
